<h1 class="article-title">This Year’s Grain Season Starts Later Than Usual at the Port of Liepāja</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>In August 2026, stevedoring companies at the Port of Liepāja handled 341.83 thousand tonnes of various cargoes. Dry bulk traditionally represented the largest category, including agricultural products, building materials and wood chips, at 188.16 thousand tonnes. General cargo was the second-largest group at 153.24 thousand tonnes, of which 129.2 thousand tonnes were carried on the regular Liepāja–Travemünde ferry service to Germany.</p><p>The volume of cargo handled in August 2026 was lower than the average for the same period in other years.*</p><p>2026 – 84.01 thousand tonnes; 2025 – 161.7 thousand tonnes; 2024 – 184.7 thousand tonnes; 2023 – 212.6 thousand tonnes; 2026 – 217.2 thousand tonnes.</p><p>* Grain handled in August, in thousands of tonnes, by year</p><p>Historically, and since operations resumed in the 1990s, the Port of Liepāja has deliberately developed its grain terminals, emphasising local cargoes (around 50% of the total, with the remainder being transit cargo), including exports of grain grown in Latvia. In early August 2026, grain terminals at the port began accepting Latvian barley and wheat under existing contracts. Their combined simultaneous storage capacity is approximately 730 thousand tonnes.</p><p>The largest dry bulk terminal at the Port of Liepāja, LSEZ SIA Liepaja Bulk Terminal LTD, has signed contracts with leading Latvian grain exporters, including Baltic Agro, Latraps and Linas Agro, for the period from 1 July 2026 to 1 July 2027. The company also works with international businesses such as Archer Daniels Midland (ADM). According to its board chair, Juris Matvejevs, Latvian grain is arriving more slowly than in previous years. The first export shipment of Latvian wheat left Liepāja only on 6 September, when the Panamax-class vessel “Tuo Fu 8”, carrying 65,486.39 tonnes, departed for Abu Dhabi. The terminal also works with Central Asian countries, exporting agricultural products to EU countries and importing Argentine soybeans and Brazilian sugar. Its partners include ADM (USA), one of the world’s leading traders and processors of agricultural commodities. ADM purchases, stores, transports and processes grain and oilseeds, connecting farmers to food, feed and industrial markets worldwide.</p><p>The second-largest grain terminal, DanStore, was established in 1999 as a joint venture between the Danish companies Svane Shipping and Scanmills. In 2003, the international agricultural commodity trader and processor Bunge became a strategic partner, helping create and develop the specialised dry bulk terminal. DanStore currently operates under contracts that include Latvian grain companies Baltic Agro and Agerona. Wheat grown by Latvian farmers is now being loaded aboard the Handysize-class vessel “Jera” for delivery to Nigeria.</p><p>Local cargoes have always been a priority for the Port of Liepāja’s stevedoring companies. However, to ensure even use of handling capacity, they also contract with the world’s largest freight logistics operators. Approximately half of the port’s cargo originates in Latvia, while the other half is transit cargo. In recent years, considerable effort has gone into developing a transit corridor with Central Asian countries. If grain transit to third countries is restricted, global grain—and therefore food—prices could rise sharply, potentially contributing to inflation in Europe, including Latvia, and worldwide.</p><p> </p></div>
